# Lifeblood speech: how the young sound. The format and every fact and word are described in basics.speech.
#
# A symbol with an age stage after a dot ("greet.child") replaces the plain one for speakers at that stage:
#   baby        0-2   babbling and baby talk. Anything without a .baby version comes out of "babble".
#   child       3-9   little kids
#   young_teen  10-15 early teens
#   teen        16-17 late teens
# If nothing in the stage's version fits, the plain symbol is used (except for babies), so keep plain lines in each.
# The age fact holds the stage (baby/child/young_teen/teen/adult), and so does listener_age for who they talk to.

# ===============================================================================================================
# Babies (0-2)

== babble ==
Ba!
Ba-ba-ba.
Da!
Goo.
Gah!
Bwah!
Mmm-ba.
Ah-ah-ah!
Pbbbt.
Eee!
Heehee!                      {lighthearted, w=2}
Hee! Ba!                     {extrovert, w=2}
...                          {introvert, w=2}
Mm.                          {taciturn, w=2}
Hnnh.                        {serious, w=2}
Nuh!                         {rude, w=2}
Waaah!                       {mood=bad, w=3}
Waaah!                       {pessimist, w=1.5}
Hic.                         {w=0.5}
Nom nom.                     {glutton, w=3}
Doggy!                       {animal_lover, w=2}
[kin_call]!                  {listener_kin=parent, w=6}
[kin_call]! [kin_call]!      {listener_kin=parent, extrovert, w=6}
Up! Up!                      {listener_kin=parent, w=5}
Uh-oh.                       {w=0.7}
Wha dat?                     {curious, w=3}
No!                          {hothead, w=2}

